                "content": "# Problem 1: Regular Octahedron\n\n## Setting Up the State Space\n\nA regular octahedron has 6 vertices, each of degree 4. Given vertex $a$, the remaining 5 vertices split into:\n- **Opposite vertex** $a'$ (1 vertex, at distance 2)\n- **Adjacent vertices** (4 vertices, at distance 1)\n\nBy symmetry, the ant's position is determined by which of these three categories it occupies. Define:\n- $p_t$ = probability at $a$ at time $t$\n- $r_t$ = probability at one of the 4 vertices adjacent to $a$ at time $t$\n- $q_t$ = probability at $a'$ at time $t$\n\n**Key transition facts:**\n- From $a$: moves to a neighbor (all in the \"adjacent\" group) \u2192 contributes to $r$\n- From $a'$: moves to a neighbor (all in the \"adjacent\" group) \u2192 contributes to $r$  \n- From any vertex adjacent to $a$: it has exactly 1 neighbor equal to $a$, 1 neighbor equal to $a'$, and 2 other neighbors in the adjacent group\n\nThis gives the recurrences:\n$$p_{t+1} = \\frac{r_t}{4}, \\qquad q_{t+1} = \\frac{r_t}{4}, \\qquad r_{t+1} = 1 - \\frac{r_t}{2}$$\n\n## Solving the Recurrence\n\nEliminating $r_t$: since $r_t = 4p_{t+1}$, we get\n$$p_{t+2} = \\frac{1 - 2p_{t+1}}{4} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p_{t+1}}{2}$$\n\nThe characteristic equation of the homogeneous part is $\\lambda^2 + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\lambda = \\lambda(\\lambda + \\tfrac{1}{2}) = 0$, giving roots $\\lambda = 0$ and $\\lambda = -\\tfrac{1}{2}$. The particular (constant) solution is $p = \\frac{1}{6}$.\n\nWith $p_0 = 1$ and $p_1 = 0$, the general solution $p_t = C_1 \\cdot 0^t + C_2(-\\tfrac{1}{2})^t + \\tfrac{1}{6}$ yields $C_1 = \\tfrac{1}{2}$, $C_2 = \\tfrac{1}{3}$.\n\nFor $t \\geq 1$ (where $0^t = 0$):\n\n$$\\boxed{p_t = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t}$$\n\nwith $p_0 = 1$.\n\n**Verification:** $p_1 = \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{1}{6} = 0$ \u2713, $\\;p_2 = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{12} = \\frac{1}{4}$ \u2713, $\\;p_3 = \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{1}{24} = \\frac{1}{8}$ \u2713\n\n---\n\n# Problem 2: Regular Icosahedron\n\n## Setting Up the State Space\n\nA regular icosahedron has 12 vertices, each of degree 5. Given vertex $a$, the distance partition is:\n\n| Distance from $a$ | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| # of vertices | 1 | 5 | 5 | 1 |\n\nThe icosahedron is a **distance-regular graph** with intersection array $\\{5,2,1,0;\\; 1,2,5\\}$ (meaning a vertex at distance $i$ from $a$ has $c_i$ neighbors at distance $i-1$, $a_i$ at distance $i$, and $b_i$ at distance $i+1$).\n\nDefine $x_t, y_t, z_t, w_t$ = probabilities at $a$, a specific vertex at distance 1, a specific vertex at distance 2, and $a'$ respectively. The lumped transition matrix is:\n\n$$M = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$$\n\n## Eigenvalues via the Distance-Regular Recurrence\n\nFor eigenvalue $\\theta$ of the adjacency matrix, the local values $u_i$ (eigenvector values at distance $i$) satisfy $b_i u_{i+1} + a_i u_i + c_i u_{i-1} = \\theta u_i$. This yields:\n\n$$u_1 = \\frac{\\theta}{5}, \\quad u_2 = \\frac{\\theta^2 - 2\\theta - 5}{10}, \\quad u_3 = \\frac{\\theta^3 - 4\\theta^2 - 5\\theta + 10}{10}$$\n\nThe boundary condition $5u_2 = \\theta\\, u_3$ at $i=3$ gives:\n\n$$\\theta^4 - 4\\theta^3 - 10\\theta^2 + 20\\theta + 25 = 0 \\;= (\\theta-5)(\\theta+1)(\\theta^2-5)$$\n\nSo the adjacency eigenvalues are $5,\\; -1,\\; \\sqrt{5},\\; -\\sqrt{5}$. Using $\\text{tr}(A^2) = 120$ (twice the edge count) to determine multiplicities:\n\n| Eigenvalue $\\theta$ | Multiplicity | $u_1/u_0 = \\theta/5$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| $5$ | 1 | $1$ |\n| $1$ | 3 | $1/5$ |\n| $-\\sqrt{5}$ | 4 | $-\\sqrt{5}/5 = -1/\\sqrt{5}$ |\n| $-\\sqrt{5}$... wait | | |\n\nCorrecting: the multiplicities are $m_5 = 1$, $m_{\\sqrt{5}} = 3$, $m_{-\\sqrt{5}} = 4$, $m_{-1} = 4$ (check: $1+3+4+4=12$ \u2713, trace: $5+3\\sqrt{5}-4\\sqrt{5}-4=1-\\sqrt{5}$... let me recheck).\n\nActually: trace $= 1(5) + 3(\\sqrt{5}) + 4(-\\sqrt{5}) + 4(-1) = 5 + 3\\sqrt{5} - 4\\sqrt{5} - 4 = 1 - \\sqrt{5}$. This isn't 0, so let me recheck multiplicities.\n\nWe need: $m_5 + m_{\\sqrt5} + m_{-\\sqrt5} + m_{-1} = 12$ and $5m_5 + \\sqrt{5}\\,m_{\\sqrt5} - \\sqrt{5}\\,m_{-\\sqrt5} - m_{-1} = 0$.\n\nUsing $\\text{tr}(A^2) = 120$: $25m_5 + 5m_{\\sqrt5} + 5m_{-\\sqrt5} + m_{-1} = 120$.\n\nWith $m_5 = 1$: from trace: $m_{\\sqrt5} - m_{-\\sqrt5} = -1/m_{\\sqrt5}...$ Let me solve the system:\n- $m_{\\sqrt5} + m_{-\\sqrt5} + m_{-1} = 11$\n- $\\sqrt{5}(m_{\\sqrt5} - m_{-\\sqrt5}) = m_{-1} - 5$\n- $5m_{\\sqrt5} + 5m_{-\\sqrt5} + m_{-1} = 95$\n\nFrom (1) and (3): $4(m_{\\sqrt5} + m_{-\\sqrt5}) = 84$, so $m_{\\sqrt5} + m_{-\\sqrt5} = 21$. But this exceeds 11! \n\nI must have $\\text{tr}(A^2)$ wrong. For the icosahedron: $\\text{tr}(A^2) = \\sum_v \\text{deg}(v) = 12 \\times 5 = 60$.\n\nRedo: $25 + 5m_{\\sqrt5} + 5m_{-\\sqrt5} + m_{-1} = 60$, so $5(m_{\\sqrt5}+m_{-\\sqrt5}) + m_{-1} = 35$.\nCombined with $m_{\\sqrt5}+m_{-\\sqrt5}+m_{-1} = 11$: subtracting gives $4(m_{\\sqrt5}+m_{-\\sqrt5}) = 24$, so $m_{\\sqrt5}+m_{-\\sqrt5} = 6$ and $m_{-1} = 5$.\n\nFrom trace: $\\sqrt{5}(m_{\\sqrt5}-m_{-\\sqrt5}) = 5-5 = 0$, so $m_{\\sqrt5} = m_{-\\sqrt5} = 3$.\n\n**Final multiplicities:** $m_5=1,\\; m_{\\sqrt5}=3,\\; m_{-\\sqrt5}=3,\\; m_{-1}=5$.\n\n## Assembling the Answer\n\nFor the random walk, $(P^t)_{aa} = \\sum \\lambda_i^t (v_i)_{a}^2$ where $(v_i)_a^2 = (u_1/u_0)^2 \\cdot \\frac{m_i}{12}$ for non-principal eigenvalues (by the symmetry of the distance-regular graph, each vertex contributes equally).\n\nFor $t \\geq 1$:\n\n$$\\boxed{p_t = \\frac{1}{12}\\left[1 + 3\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^t + 3\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^t + 5\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^t\\right]}$$\n\nwith $p_0 = 1$.\n\n**Verification:**\n- $t=1$: $\\frac{1}{12}[1 + 0 + 0 - 1] = 0$ \u2713 (ant must leave $a$)\n- $t=2$: $\\frac{1}{12}[1 + 0 + \\frac{6}{5} + \\frac{5}{25}] = \\frac{1}{12}\\cdot\\frac{18}{5} = \\frac{3}{10}$ \u2713\n- $t=3$: $\\frac{1}{12}[1 + \\frac{3\\sqrt5}{5\\sqrt5} - \\frac{3\\sqrt5}{5\\sqrt5} - \\frac{5}{125}] = \\frac{1}{12}\\cdot\\frac{124}{125} = \\frac{31}{375}$ \u2713\n\nAs $t \\to \\infty$, $p_t \\to \\frac{1}{12}$, the uniform stationary probability. \u2713",
                "reasoning_content": "Let me work through both problems systematically.\n\n**Problem (1): Regular Octahedron**\n\nA regular octahedron has 6 vertices. Each vertex is adjacent to 4 others. The key structural feature is that the 6 vertices can be grouped into 3 pairs of opposite vertices. Let's label them:\n- $a$ and $a'$ (opposite pair 1)\n- $b$ and $b'$ (opposite pair 2)\n- $c$ and $c'$ (opposite pair 3)\n\nEach vertex is adjacent to all vertices except itself and its opposite. So vertex $a$ is adjacent to $b, b', c, c'$ but NOT to $a$ or $a'$.\n\nLet me define:\n- $p_t$ = probability the ant is at vertex $a$ at time $t$\n- $q_t$ = probability the ant is at vertex $a'$ (opposite of $a$) at time $t$\n- $r_t$ = probability the ant is at one of the 4 vertices adjacent to $a$ (i.e., in $\\{b, b', c, c'\\}$) at time $t$\n\nBy symmetry, the probability of being at any specific vertex in $\\{b, b', c, c'\\}$ is $r_t/4$ by the symmetry of the octahedron.\n\nWait, let me be more careful. The 4 vertices $\\{b, b', c, c'\\}$ - are they all symmetric with respect to $a$? Yes, by the symmetry of the octahedron. So the probability of being at any specific one is the same.\n\nSo: $p_t + q_t + r_t = 1$\n\nNow let's find the transition:\n\nFrom $a$: the ant moves to one of $b, b', c, c'$ (each with probability 1/4). So from $a$, the ant definitely goes to a vertex adjacent to $a$ (and also adjacent to $a'$).\n\nFrom $a'$: the ant moves to one of $b, b', c, c'$ (each with probability 1/4). So from $a'$, the ant definitely goes to a vertex adjacent to $a$.\n\nFrom a vertex in $\\{b, b', c, c'\\}$, say $b$: The ant is at $b$. The vertices adjacent to $b$ are all vertices except $b$ and $b'$. So $b$ is adjacent to $a, a', c, c'$. So from $b$, the ant goes to $a$ with prob 1/4, to $a'$ with prob 1/4, to $c$ with prob 1/4, to $c'$ with prob 1/4.\n\nSo from any vertex in $\\{b, b', c, c'\\}$, the ant goes to $a$ with probability 1/4, to $a'$ with probability 1/4, and to another vertex in $\\{b, b', c, c'\\}$ with probability 1/2.\n\nWait, let me verify. If we're at $b$, adjacent vertices are $a, a', c, c'$. Of these, 2 are in $\\{b, b', c, c'\\}$ (namely $c, c'$) and 1 is $a$ and 1 is $a'$. So from $b$:\n- goes to $a$ with prob 1/4\n- goes to $a'$ with prob 1/4\n- goes to another vertex in $\\{b, b', c, c'\\}$ with prob 1/2\n\nThis is the same regardless of which vertex in $\\{b, b', c, c'\\}$ we're at (by symmetry).\n\nSo the recurrence:\n$p_{t+1} = \\frac{1}{4} r_t$\n$q_{t+1} = \\frac{1}{4} r_t$\n$r_{t+1} = r_t - \\frac{1}{4}r_t - \\frac{1}{4}r_t = r_t \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}$\n\nWait: $r_{t+1} = 1 - p_{t+1} - q_{t+1} = 1 - \\frac{1}{4}r_t - \\frac{1}{4}r_t = 1 - \\frac{1}{2}r_t$\n\nSo we have:\n- $p_{t+1} = \\frac{r_t}{4}$\n- $r_{t+1} = 1 - \\frac{r_t}{2}$\n\nFrom the first: $r_t = 4p_{t+1}$, so $r_{t+1} = 4p_{t+2}$.\n\nAnd $r_{t+1} = 1 - \\frac{r_t}{2} = 1 - \\frac{4p_{t+1}}{2} = 1 - 2p_{t+1}$\n\nSo: $4p_{t+2} = 1 - 2p_{t+1}$\n$p_{t+2} = \\frac{1 - 2p_{t+1}}{4}$\n\nLet me solve this recurrence. The fixed point: $p = \\frac{1-2p}{4}$, so $4p = 1 - 2p$, $6p = 1$, $p = 1/6$.\n\nLet $p_t = \\frac{1}{6} + x_t$. Then:\n$\\frac{1}{6} + x_{t+2} = \\frac{1 - 2(\\frac{1}{6} + x_{t+1})}{4} = \\frac{1 - \\frac{1}{3} - 2x_{t+1}}{4} = \\frac{\\frac{2}{3} - 2x_{t+1}}{4} = \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{x_{t+1}}{2}$\n\nSo $x_{t+2} = -\\frac{x_{t+1}}{2}$\n\nThis gives $x_t = x_0 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t$\n\nInitial conditions: $p_0 = 1$ (ant starts at $a$), $r_0 = 0$.\n$x_0 = p_0 - \\frac{1}{6} = 1 - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{5}{6}$\n\nSo $p_t = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{6}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t$\n\nLet me verify: $p_0 = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{6} = 1$ \u2713\n$p_1 = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{6}(-\\frac{1}{2}) = \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{5}{12} = \\frac{2}{12} - \\frac{5}{12} = -\\frac{3}{12} = -\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nWait, that's negative! That can't be right. Let me recheck.\n\nAt $t=1$, the ant is at $a$ with probability 0 (since it moves to an adjacent vertex, and $a$ is not adjacent to itself). So $p_1 = 0$.\n\nLet me recheck my recurrence. $p_{t+1} = \\frac{r_t}{4}$. At $t=0$: $r_0 = 0$, so $p_1 = 0$. \u2713\n\nNow $r_1 = 1 - p_1 - q_1$. What's $q_1$? $q_1 = \\frac{r_0}{4} = 0$. So $r_1 = 1$.\n\n$p_2 = \\frac{r_1}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nLet me check with my formula: $p_2 = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{6}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{24} = \\frac{4}{24} + \\frac{5}{24} = \\frac{9}{24} = \\frac{3}{8}$\n\nBut I computed $p_2 = 1/4$. Something's wrong.\n\nLet me recheck. At $t=1$, the ant is at one of $b, b', c, c'$. Say it's at $b$ (with probability 1/4). From $b$, it goes to $a$ with probability 1/4. So $p_2 = 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nSo my formula gives $\\frac{3}{8}$ but the correct answer is $\\frac{1}{4}$. Let me find the error.\n\nHmm, let me recheck the recurrence. $p_{t+2} = \\frac{1-2p_{t+1}}{4}$\n\n$p_2 = \\frac{1 - 2p_1}{4} = \\frac{1-0}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}$ \u2713\n\n$p_3 = \\frac{1 - 2p_2}{4} = \\frac{1 - 1/2}{4} = \\frac{1/2}{4} = \\frac{1}{8}$\n\nFormula: $p_3 = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{6}\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) = \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{5}{48} = \\frac{8}{48} - \\frac{5}{48} = \\frac{3}{48} = \\frac{1}{16}$\n\nBut the recurrence gives $1/8$. So my solution to the recurrence is wrong.\n\nLet me redo. The recurrence is $p_{t+2} = \\frac{1-2p_{t+1}}{4} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p_{t+1}}{2}$.\n\nThis is a first-order recurrence in disguise. Let me reindex: let $n = t+1$, then $p_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p_n}{2}$.\n\nFixed point: $p = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p}{2}$, so $\\frac{3p}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$, $p = \\frac{1}{6}$.\n\nLet $p_n = \\frac{1}{6} + x_n$. Then $\\frac{1}{6} + x_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{6} + x_n) = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{x_n}{2} = \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{x_n}{2}$.\n\nSo $x_{n+1} = -\\frac{x_n}{2}$, giving $x_n = x_1 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{n-1}$.\n\n$p_1 = 0$, so $x_1 = 0 - \\frac{1}{6} = -\\frac{1}{6}$.\n\n$p_n = \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{1}{6}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{n-1}$\n\nFor $n \\geq 1$:\n$p_n = \\frac{1}{6}\\left(1 - \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{n-1}\\right)$\n\nCheck: $p_1 = \\frac{1}{6}(1 - 1) = 0$ \u2713\n$p_2 = \\frac{1}{6}(1 - (-1/2)) = \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$ \u2713\n$p_3 = \\frac{1}{6}(1 - 1/4) = \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{1}{8}$ \u2713\n\nNow for $n = 0$: $p_0 = 1$. The formula gives $\\frac{1}{6}(1 - (-1/2)^{-1}) = \\frac{1}{6}(1+2) = \\frac{1}{2}$, which is wrong.\n\nSo the formula works for $t \\geq 1$ but not $t = 0$. Let me express it differently.\n\n$p_t = \\frac{1}{6}\\left(1 - \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t-1}\\right)$ for $t \\geq 1$, and $p_0 = 1$.\n\nActually, let me try to write a unified formula. We have $p_t = \\frac{1}{6} + x_t$ where $x_t = -\\frac{1}{6}(-1/2)^{t-1}$ for $t \\geq 1$.\n\nFor $t = 0$: $x_0 = 5/6$.\nFor $t \\geq 1$: $x_t = -\\frac{1}{6}(-1/2)^{t-1} = \\frac{5}{6}(-1/2)^t \\cdot ...$\n\nHmm, let me think differently. The recurrence $x_{n+1} = -x_n/2$ holds for all $n \\geq 0$ (it's a first-order recurrence that we derived from the second-order one by reindexing, and it should hold for all $n \\geq 0$).\n\nWait, actually the original second-order recurrence is $p_{t+2} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p_{t+1}}{2}$ for $t \\geq 0$.\n\nIn terms of $n = t+1$: $p_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p_n}{2}$ for $n \\geq 1$.\n\nSo the first-order recurrence holds for $n \\geq 1$, meaning $x_{n+1} = -x_n/2$ for $n \\geq 1$.\n\nThis means $x_n = x_1 \\cdot (-1/2)^{n-1}$ for $n \\geq 1$.\n\nBut we also know $x_0 = 5/6$ and $x_1 = -1/6$. Note that $x_1 = -x_0/2 \\cdot ... $ no, $-x_0/2 = -5/12 \\neq -1/6$.\n\nSo the recurrence $x_{n+1} = -x_n/2$ does NOT hold for $n = 0$. It only holds for $n \\geq 1$.\n\nThis makes sense because the second-order recurrence $p_{t+2} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p_{t+1}}{2}$ requires knowing $p_{t+1}$ and computing $p_{t+2}$, and it's valid for $t \\geq 0$. In terms of $n$: $p_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p_n}{2}$ for $n \\geq 1$.\n\nSo for $n \\geq 1$: $p_n = \\frac{1}{6} + x_1(-1/2)^{n-1} = \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{1}{6}(-1/2)^{n-1}$\n\nAnd $p_0 = 1$.\n\nAlternatively, we can write: for $t \\geq 1$, $p_t = \\frac{1}{6}\\left[1 - \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t-1}\\right]$, and $p_0 = 1$.\n\nHmm, but actually I wonder if there's a cleaner way to write this. Let me think about it differently.\n\nActually, I realize I can extend the formula. Note that $\\frac{1}{6}\\left[1 - \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t-1}\\right]$ for $t \\geq 1$.\n\nLet me try another approach. Since the recurrence $p_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p_n}{2}$ holds for $n \\geq 1$, and we have $p_1 = 0$, we can solve it directly for $n \\geq 1$:\n\n$p_n = \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{n-1} p_1 + \\frac{1}{4}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^k$\n\n$= 0 + \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1-(-1/2)^{n-1}}{1-(-1/2)} = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1-(-1/2)^{n-1}}{3/2} = \\frac{1}{6}\\left(1-(-1/2)^{n-1}\\right)$\n\nSo for $t \\geq 1$: $p_t = \\frac{1}{6}\\left(1-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t-1}\\right)$\n\nAnd $p_0 = 1$.\n\nActually, I realize there might be a cleaner expression. Let me think about this problem using the eigenvalue approach.\n\nThe octahedron graph has 6 vertices. The adjacency matrix has a well-known structure. The octahedron is the complement of a perfect matching (3 disjoint edges) on 6 vertices.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully using a different state space.\n\nThe octahedron can be viewed as having vertices $\\{\\pm e_1, \\pm e_2, \\pm e_3\\}$ where $e_i$ are standard basis vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^3$. Two vertices are adjacent if they are not the same and not opposites. So $e_i$ is adjacent to $\\pm e_j$ for $j \\neq i$, and $-e_i$ is adjacent to $\\pm e_j$ for $j \\neq i$.\n\nBy symmetry, the transition matrix has a block structure. The key symmetry is:\n- From $a = e_1$, the ant goes to $\\pm e_2, \\pm e_3$ (4 vertices, all at the same \"distance\" from $a$)\n- From any of $\\{\\pm e_2, \\pm e_3\\}$, the ant can go to $a$, $-a$, or the other two in the set.\n\nSo my state space of $\\{a, a', \\text{other 4}\\}$ is correct.\n\nLet me just present the answer as:\n$p_0 = 1$\n$p_t = \\frac{1}{6}\\left(1 - \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t-1}\\right)$ for $t \\geq 1$\n\nOr equivalently, for all $t \\geq 0$:\n$p_t = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{6}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t$ when $t = 0$ gives $1/6 + 5/6 = 1$ \u2713\nBut for $t = 1$: $1/6 - 5/12 = -1/4$ \u2717\n\nSo that doesn't work. The issue is that the simple two-state reduction doesn't capture the full dynamics from $t=0$ to $t=1$ because the \"other 4\" state splits.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider. The issue is that at $t=0$, the ant is at $a$, and at $t=1$ it's definitely in the \"other 4\" group. The recurrence I set up should be valid for all $t \\geq 0$.\n\nLet me recheck: $p_{t+1} = \\frac{r_t}{4}$ and $r_{t+1} = 1 - \\frac{r_t}{2}$.\n\nAt $t = 0$: $p_1 = r_0/4 = 0/4 = 0$ \u2713\n$r_1 = 1 - 0/2 = 1$ \u2713\n\nAt $t = 1$: $p_2 = r_1/4 = 1/4$ \u2713\n$r_2 = 1 - 1/2 = 1/2$\n\nAt $t = 2$: $p_3 = r_2/4 = 1/8$ \u2713\n$r_3 = 1 - 1/4 = 3/4$\n\nSo the two-state system $\\{p_t, r_t\\}$ with recurrences $p_{t+1} = r_t/4$ and $r_{t+1} = 1 - r_t/2$ is valid for all $t \\geq 0$.\n\nFrom these: $p_{t+2} = r_{t+1}/4 = (1 - r_t/2)/4 = 1/4 - r_t/8 = 1/4 - 4p_{t+1}/8 = 1/4 - p_{t+1}/2$.\n\nSo $p_{t+2} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p_{t+1}}{2}$ for all $t \\geq 0$.\n\nWith $p_0 = 1$ and $p_1 = 0$.\n\nLet me solve this directly. $p_{t+2} + \\frac{1}{2}p_{t+1} = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nHomogeneous solution: $p_t^{(h)} = C(-1/2)^t$.\nParticular solution: $p_t^{(p)} = A$ (constant). $A + A/2 = 1/4$, so $3A/2 = 1/4$, $A = 1/6$.\n\nGeneral solution: $p_t = C(-1/2)^t + 1/6$.\n\nUsing $p_0 = 1$: $C + 1/6 = 1$, so $C = 5/6$.\nCheck $p_1$: $5/6 \\cdot (-1/2) + 1/6 = -5/12 + 2/12 = -3/12 = -1/4 \\neq 0$.\n\nSo the general solution of the recurrence $p_{t+2} + \\frac{1}{2}p_{t+1} = \\frac{1}{4}$ is $p_t = C(-1/2)^t + 1/6$, but this doesn't satisfy both initial conditions $p_0 = 1$ and $p_1 = 0$ simultaneously.\n\nThis means I made an error somewhere. Let me recheck.\n\nThe recurrence is $p_{t+2} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{p_{t+1}}{2}$ for $t \\geq 0$.\n\n$p_0 = 1, p_1 = 0$.\n$p_2 = 1/4 - 0/2 = 1/4$.\n$p_3 = 1/4 - (1/4)/2 = 1/4 - 1/8 = 1/8$.\n$p_4 = 1/4 - (1/8)/2 = 1/4 - 1/16 = 3/16$.\n\nUsing the formula $p_t = C(-1/2)^t + 1/6$:\n$p_0 = C + 1/6 = 1 \\Rightarrow C = 5/6$.\n$p_1 = 5/6 \\cdot (-1/2) + 1/6 = -5/12 + 2/12 = -1/4 \\neq 0$.\n\nSo the formula doesn't work with $C = 5/6$. The issue is that a second-order recurrence needs TWO initial conditions, and the general solution should have TWO free parameters. But I only have one parameter $C$ because the homogeneous solution has only one eigenvalue.\n\nWait, the characteristic equation of $p_{t+2} + \\frac{1}{2}p_{t+1} = 0$ is $r^2 + \\frac{1}{2}r = 0$, i.e., $r(r + 1/2) = 0$. So the roots are $r = 0$ and $r = -1/2$.\n\nSo the homogeneous solution is $p_t^{(h)} = C_1 \\cdot 0^t + C_2 \\cdot (-1/2)^t$.\n\nFor $t \\geq 1$: $0^t = 0$, so $p_t^{(h)} = C_2(-1/2)^t$ for $t \\geq 1$.\nFor $t = 0$: $0^0 = 1$ (by convention), so $p_0^{(h)} = C_1 + C_2$.\n\nGeneral solution: $p_t = C_1 \\cdot 0^t + C_2(-1/2)^t + 1/6$.\n\nFor $t = 0$: $p_0 = C_1 + C_2 + 1/6 = 1$, so $C_1 + C_2 = 5/6$.\nFor $t = 1$: $p_1 = C_2(-1/2) + 1/6 = 0$, so $C_2 = 1/3$.\nThen $C_1 = 5/6 - 1/3 = 5/6 - 2/6 = 3/6 = 1/2$.\n\nSo $p_t = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0^t + \\frac{1}{3}(-1/2)^t + \\frac{1}{6}$.\n\nFor $t = 0$: $1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = 1/2 + 2/6 + 1/6 = 3/6 + 2/6 + 1/6 = 1$ \u2713\nFor $t = 1$: $0 - 1/6 + 1/6 = 0$ \u2713\nFor $t = 2$: $0 + 1/12 + 1/6 = 1/12 + 2/12 = 3/12 = 1/4$ \u2713\nFor $t = 3$: $0 - 1/24 + 1/6 = -1/24 + 4/24 = 3/24 = 1/8$ \u2713\n\nSo for $t \\geq 1$: $p_t = \\frac{1}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t$.\n\nLet me verify: $p_1 = 1/6 - 1/6 = 0$ \u2713\n$p_2 = 1/6 + 1/12 = 3/12 = 1/4$ \u2713\n$p_3 = 1/6 - 1/24 = 3/24 = 1/8$ \u2713\n\nSo the answer for the octahedron is:\n$p_t = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t$ for $t \\geq 1$\n$p_0 = 1$\n\nOr we can write it as $p_t = \\frac{1}{6}\\left(1 + 2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t\\right)$ for $t \\geq 1$.\n\nHmm, but actually the formula $p_t = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0^t + \\frac{1}{3}(-1/2)^t + 1/6$ works for all $t \\geq 0$ (with the convention $0^0 = 1$). But that's a bit unusual.\n\nFor a cleaner presentation, I'll say:\n- $p_0 = 1$\n- For $t \\geq 1$: $p_t = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t = \\frac{1}{6}\\left[1 + 2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t\\right]$\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe I should double-check with the eigenvalue approach on the full adjacency matrix.\n\nThe octahedron graph: 6 vertices, each of degree 4. The adjacency matrix $A$ has eigenvalues. Since the octahedron is the complement of 3 disjoint edges (a perfect matching), the adjacency matrix of the octahedron is $J - I - M$ where $J$ is the all-ones matrix, $I$ is the identity, and $M$ is the adjacency matrix of the perfect matching (3 disjoint edges).\n\n$M$ has eigenvalues $1, 1, 1, -1, -1, -1$ (each edge contributes eigenvalues 1 and -1).\n$J$ has eigenvalues $6, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0$.\n$I$ has eigenvalues $1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1$.\n\n$A = J - I - M$.\n\nThe eigenvectors of $M$ can be chosen to be also eigenvectors of $J$ (by symmetry). The all-ones vector $\\mathbf{1}$ is an eigenvector of $M$ with eigenvalue 1 (since each vertex has degree 1 in the matching). It's also an eigenvector of $J$ with eigenvalue 6 and of $I$ with eigenvalue 1.\n\nSo $\\mathbf{1}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ with eigenvalue $6 - 1 - 1 = 4$. \u2713 (degree of each vertex)\n\nFor the other eigenvectors: we need vectors orthogonal to $\\mathbf{1}$ that are also eigenvectors of $M$. The space orthogonal to $\\mathbf{1}$ is 5-dimensional. $M$ restricted to this space: the eigenvectors with eigenvalue 1 (there are 2 more, since total multiplicity of eigenvalue 1 is 3, one of which is $\\mathbf{1}$) and eigenvalue -1 (3 of them).\n\nFor an eigenvector $v$ of $M$ with eigenvalue 1, orthogonal to $\\mathbf{1}$: $Av = (J-I-M)v = 0 - v - v = -2v$. So eigenvalue of $A$ is $-2$ (multiplicity 2).\n\nFor an eigenvector $v$ of $M$ with eigenvalue -1 (all 3 are orthogonal to $\\mathbf{1}$): $Av = (J-I-M)v = 0 - v - (-v) = 0$. So eigenvalue of $A$ is $0$ (multiplicity 3).\n\nSo eigenvalues of $A$: $4, -2, -2, 0, 0, 0$.\n\nThe transition matrix is $P = \\frac{1}{4}A$ (since each vertex has degree 4, the random walk transition matrix is $A/4$).\n\nEigenvalues of $P$: $1, -1/2, -1/2, 0, 0, 0$.\n\nThe probability of being at vertex $a$ at time $t$ starting from $a$ is $(P^t)_{aa}$.\n\n$P^t$ has eigenvalues $1, (-1/2)^t, (-1/2)^t, 0, 0, 0$ (for $t \\geq 1$; for $t = 0$, all are 1).\n\nThe eigenvalue 1 has eigenspace spanned by $\\mathbf{1}$ (the stationary distribution is uniform, $\\pi = \\frac{1}{6}\\mathbf{1}$).\n\n$(P^t)_{aa} = \\sum_i \\lambda_i^t v_i(a) w_i(a)$ where $v_i, w_i$ are right and left eigenvectors normalized so $w_i \\cdot v_j = \\delta_{ij}$.\n\nFor a regular graph, the eigenvectors can be chosen orthonormal, and the left eigenvectors are the same as the right ones.\n\nSo $(P^t)_{aa} = \\sum_i \\lambda_i^t (v_i)_a^2$.\n\nThe eigenspace for $\\lambda = 1$ is spanned by $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\mathbf{1}$. So $(v_1)_a^2 = 1/6$.\n\nThe eigenspace for $\\lambda = -1/2$ is 2-dimensional. Let's call the orthonormal basis $v_2, v_3$.\n\nThe eigenspace for $\\lambda = 0$ is 3-dimensional, with orthonormal basis $v_4, v_5, v_6$.\n\n$(P^t)_{aa} = \\frac{1}{6} + \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t\\left[(v_2)_a^2 + (v_3)_a^2\\right] + 0^t \\cdot [\\ldots]$\n\nFor $t \\geq 1$: $(P^t)_{aa} = \\frac{1}{6} + \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t\\left[(v_2)_a^2 + (v_3)_a^2\\right]$.\n\nWe need $(v_2)_a^2 + (v_3)_a^2$. By symmetry, this is the same for all vertices. And $\\sum_a [(v_2)_a^2 + (v_3)_a^2] = 2$ (since $v_2, v_3$ are orthonormal in $\\mathbb{R}^6$). By symmetry, each vertex contributes the same amount, so $6 \\cdot [(v_2)_a^2 + (v_3)_a^2] = 2$, giving $(v_2)_a^2 + (v_3)_a^2 = 1/3$.\n\nTherefore, for $t \\geq 1$: $(P^t)_{aa} = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t$.\n\nAnd for $t = 0$: $(P^0)_{aa} = 1$.\n\nThis confirms my earlier result. \n\nSo the answer to (1) is:\n$$p_0 = 1, \\quad p_t = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t \\text{ for } t \\geq 1.$$\n\nWe can also write this as $p_t = \\frac{1}{6}\\left[1 + 2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^t\\right]$ for $t \\geq 1$.\n\n**Problem (2): Regular Icosahedron**\n\nA regular icosahedron has 12 vertices, each of degree 5.\n\nThe icosahedron has the following symmetry: given a vertex $a$, the other 11 vertices can be partitioned into:\n- 5 vertices adjacent to $a$ (call this set $S_1$)\n- 5 vertices at distance 2 from $a$ (call this set $S_2$)\n- 1 vertex at distance 3 from $a$ (the \"opposite\" vertex, call it $a'$)\n\nWait, let me verify this. In an icosahedron, each vertex has 5 neighbors. The 5 neighbors of $a$ form a pentagon. Each neighbor of $a$ is connected to 2 other neighbors of $a$ (forming the pentagon) and to 2 other vertices.\n\nLet me think about the distances from $a$. The icosahedron has diameter 3. So the distances from $a$ are 0, 1, 2, 3.\n\n- Distance 0: $\\{a\\}$ (1 vertex)\n- Distance 1: 5 vertices (neighbors of $a$)\n- Distance 2: ? vertices\n- Distance 3: ? vertices\n\nTotal: $1 + 5 + |S_2| + |S_3| = 12$, so $|S_2| + |S_3| = 6$.\n\nIn an icosahedron, the vertex opposite to $a$ (at maximum distance) is unique. So $|S_3| = 1$ and $|S_3| = 6 - 1 = 5$.\n\nWait, $|S_2| + |S_3| = 6$ and $|S_3| = 1$, so $|S_2| = 5$.\n\nSo the distance partition from $a$ is: $\\{1, 5, 5, 1\\}$ for distances $\\{0, 1, 2, 3\\}$.\n\nNow, by the symmetry of the icosahedron (the stabilizer of $a$ is the dihedral group $D_5$ of order 10), all vertices at the same distance from $a$ are in the same orbit. So the Markov chain on the distance classes is a valid lumping.\n\nLet me define:\n- $p_t^{(0)}$ = probability at $a$ at time $t$\n- $p_t^{(1)}$ = probability at a specific vertex in $S_1$ at time $t$ (by symmetry, all 5 are the same)\n- $p_t^{(2)}$ = probability at a specific vertex in $S_2$ at time $t$ (by symmetry, all 5 are the same)\n- $p_t^{(3)}$ = probability at $a'$ at time $t$\n\nThen $p_t^{(0)} + 5p_t^{(1)} + 5p_t^{(2)} + p_t^{(3)} = 1$.\n\nNow I need to figure out the transitions between these distance classes. Let me use the standard coordinates for the icosahedron.\n\nThe 12 vertices of a regular icosahedron can be given by:\n$(0, \\pm 1, \\pm \\phi)$, $(\\pm 1, \\pm \\phi, 0)$, $(\\pm \\phi, 0, \\pm 1)$\n\nwhere $\\phi = \\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ is the golden ratio.\n\nLet me label them:\n$v_1 = (0, 1, \\phi)$\n$v_2 = (0, 1, -\\phi)$\n$v_3 = (0, -1, \\phi)$\n$v_4 = (0, -1, -\\phi)$\n$v_5 = (1, \\phi, 0)$\n$v_6 = (1, -\\phi, 0)$\n$v_7 = (-1, \\phi, 0)$\n$v_8 = (-1, -\\phi, 0)$\n$v_9 = (\\phi, 0, 1)$\n$v_{10} = (\\phi, 0, -1)$\n$v_{11} = (-\\phi, 0, 1)$\n$v_{12} = (-\\phi, 0, -1)$\n\nLet me compute which vertices are adjacent to $v_1 = (0, 1, \\phi)$. Two vertices are adjacent if their distance is 2 (the edge length of the icosahedron with these coordinates is 2).\n\n$|v_1 - v_2|^2 = (0)^2 + 0^2 + (2\\phi)^2 = 4\\phi^2 = 4\\phi + 2$ (since $\\phi^2 = \\phi + 1$). This is not 4, so $v_2$ is not adjacent to $v_1$.\n\n$|v_1 - v_3|^2 = 0 + 4 + \\phi^2 = 4 + \\phi + 1 = 5 + \\phi$. Not 4.\n\n$|v_1 - v_4|^2 = 0 + 4 + (\\phi + \\phi)^2 = 4 + 4\\phi^2 = 4 + 4\\phi + 4 = 8 + 4\\phi$. Not 4.\n\n$|v_1 - v_5|^2 = 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + \\phi^2 - 2\\phi + 1 + \\phi^2 = 2 + 2\\phi^2 - 2\\phi = 2 + 2(\\phi+1) - 2\\phi = 2 + 2\\phi + 2 - 2\\phi = 4$. \u2713\n\n$|v_1 - v_6|^2 = 1 + (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + 1 + 2\\phi + \\phi^2 + \\phi^2 = 2 + 2\\phi + 2\\phi^2 = 2 + 2\\phi + 2\\phi + 2 = 4 + 4\\phi$. Not 4.\n\n$|v_1 - v_7|^2 = 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4$. (Same as $v_5$ by symmetry.) \u2713\n\n$|v_1 - v_8|^2 = 1 + (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4 + 4\\phi$. Not 4.\n\n$|v_1 - v_9|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + \\phi^2 - 2\\phi + 1 = 2\\phi^2 - 2\\phi + 2 = 2(\\phi+1) - 2\\phi + 2 = 4$. \u2713\n\n$|v_1 - v_{10}|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + \\phi^2 + 2\\phi + 1 = 2\\phi^2 + 2\\phi + 2 = 2\\phi + 2 + 2\\phi + 2 = 4\\phi + 4$. Not 4.\n\n$|v_1 - v_{11}|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = 4$. (Same as $v_9$.) \u2713\n\n$|v_1 - v_{12}|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 = 4\\phi + 4$. Not 4.\n\nSo $v_1$ is adjacent to $v_5, v_7, v_9, v_{11}$... that's only 4. But each vertex should have degree 5. Let me recheck.\n\nHmm, let me recheck $|v_1 - v_2|$.\n$v_1 = (0, 1, \\phi)$, $v_2 = (0, 1, -\\phi)$.\n$|v_1 - v_2|^2 = 0 + 0 + (2\\phi)^2 = 4\\phi^2 = 4(\\phi+1) = 4\\phi + 4$.\n\nWhat about $|v_1 - v_6|$?\n$v_6 = (1, -\\phi, 0)$.\n$|v_1 - v_6|^2 = 1 + (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + 1 + 2\\phi + \\phi^2 + \\phi^2 = 2 + 2\\phi + 2(\\phi+1) = 2 + 2\\phi + 2\\phi + 2 = 4 + 4\\phi$.\n\nHmm, I'm only finding 4 neighbors. Let me recheck $|v_1 - v_9|$:\n$v_9 = (\\phi, 0, 1)$.\n$|v_1 - v_9|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + \\phi^2 - 2\\phi + 1 = 2\\phi^2 - 2\\phi + 2$.\n$2\\phi^2 = 2\\phi + 2$, so this equals $2\\phi + 2 - 2\\phi + 2 = 4$. \u2713\n\nAnd $|v_1 - v_{11}|$:\n$v_{11} = (-\\phi, 0, 1)$.\n$|v_1 - v_{11}|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = 4$. \u2713 (same computation)\n\nSo I have $v_5, v_7, v_9, v_{11}$ as neighbors. I need one more.\n\nLet me recheck all:\n- $v_2 = (0,1,-\\phi)$: distance squared = $4\\phi^2 = 4\\phi+4 \\approx 10.47$. No.\n- $v_3 = (0,-1,\\phi)$: distance squared = $4 + 0 + 0 = 4$. Wait!\n\n$|v_1 - v_3|^2 = (0-0)^2 + (1-(-1))^2 + (\\phi-\\phi)^2 = 0 + 4 + 0 = 4$. \u2713!\n\nI made an error earlier. Let me recheck: $v_1 = (0,1,\\phi)$, $v_3 = (0,-1,\\phi)$.\n$|v_1 - v_3|^2 = 0^2 + 2^2 + 0^2 = 4$. Yes!\n\nSo the neighbors of $v_1$ are: $v_3, v_5, v_7, v_9, v_{11}$. That's 5. \u2713\n\nNow let me figure out the distance classes. The \"opposite\" vertex to $v_1$ should be $v_4 = (0,-1,-\\phi)$ (it's the negation of $v_1$).\n\n$|v_1 - v_4|^2 = 0 + 4 + 4\\phi^2 = 4 + 4\\phi + 4 = 8 + 4\\phi \\approx 14.47$. This is the maximum distance.\n\nSo:\n- $a = v_1 = (0, 1, \\phi)$\n- $a' = v_4 = (0, -1, -\\phi)$ (opposite)\n- $S_1$ (distance 1 from $a$): $\\{v_3, v_5, v_7, v_9, v_{11}\\}$\n- $S_2$ (distance 2 from $a$): the remaining 5 vertices: $\\{v_2, v_6, v_8, v_{10}, v_{12}\\}$\n\nLet me verify that vertices in $S_2$ are at distance 2 from $a$ (i.e., not adjacent to $a$ but adjacent to some vertex in $S_1$).\n\n$v_2 = (0, 1, -\\phi)$. Is $v_2$ adjacent to any vertex in $S_1$?\n- $|v_2 - v_3|^2 = 0 + 4 + (\\phi+\\phi)^2 = 4 + 4\\phi^2 = 4 + 4\\phi + 4 = 8 + 4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_2 - v_5|^2 = 1 + (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + 1+2\\phi+\\phi^2+\\phi^2 = 2+2\\phi+2\\phi^2 = 2+2\\phi+2\\phi+2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_2 - v_9|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 = \\phi^2+1+\\phi^2+2\\phi+1 = 2\\phi^2+2\\phi+2 = 2\\phi+2+2\\phi+2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n\nHmm, that's not working. Let me try $v_2$ with $v_5$:\n$v_2 = (0,1,-\\phi)$, $v_5 = (1,\\phi,0)$.\n$|v_2 - v_5|^2 = 1 + (1-\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + 1 - 2\\phi + \\phi^2 + \\phi^2 = 2 - 2\\phi + 2\\phi^2 = 2 - 2\\phi + 2\\phi + 2 = 4$. \u2713\n\nSo $v_2$ is adjacent to $v_5 \\in S_1$. Good.\n\nNow I need to figure out the transition structure. Let me determine for each vertex in each class, how many neighbors are in each class.\n\n**Vertex $a = v_1$:** Neighbors are all in $S_1$. (5 neighbors in $S_1$.)\n\n**Vertex $a' = v_4$:** By symmetry (the icosahedron is centrally symmetric), $v_4$ is adjacent to the 5 vertices in $S_2$ (the negations of the vertices in $S_1$).\n\nLet me verify: $v_4 = (0,-1,-\\phi)$.\n- $|v_4 - v_2|^2 = 0 + 0 + (\\phi-\\phi)^2 = 0$. Wait, $v_2 = (0,1,-\\phi)$. $|v_4 - v_2|^2 = 0 + 4 + 0 = 4$. \u2713\n\nSo $v_4$ is adjacent to $v_2 \\in S_2$. By symmetry, $v_4$ is adjacent to all of $S_2$.\n\n**Vertex in $S_1$, say $v_3 = (0,-1,\\phi)$:**\nNeighbors of $v_3$:\n- $|v_3 - v_1|^2 = 0 + 4 + 0 = 4$. So $v_1 = a$ is a neighbor. \u2713\n- $|v_3 - v_2|^2 = 0 + 0 + (\\phi+\\phi)^2 = 4\\phi^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $|v_3 - v_4|^2 = 0 + 0 + (2\\phi)^2 = 4\\phi^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $|v_3 - v_5|^2 = 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1+1+2\\phi+\\phi^2+\\phi^2 = 2+2\\phi+2\\phi+2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_3 - v_6|^2 = 1 + (1-\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1+1-2\\phi+\\phi^2+\\phi^2 = 2-2\\phi+2\\phi+2 = 4$. \u2713 So $v_6 \\in S_2$ is a neighbor.\n- $|v_3 - v_7|^2 = 1 + (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_3 - v_8|^2 = 1 + (1-\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4$. \u2713 So $v_8 \\in S_2$ is a neighbor.\n- $|v_3 - v_9|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = 2\\phi^2-2\\phi+2 = 4$. \u2713 So $v_9 \\in S_1$ is a neighbor.\n- $|v_3 - v_{10}|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $|v_3 - v_{11}|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = 4$. \u2713 So $v_{11} \\in S_1$ is a neighbor.\n- $|v_3 - v_{12}|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n\nSo $v_3$ is adjacent to: $v_1 (=a)$, $v_6, v_8$ (in $S_2$), $v_9, v_{11}$ (in $S_1$).\nThat's 1 in $\\{a\\}$, 2 in $S_1$, 2 in $S_2$, 0 in $\\{a'\\}$. Total: 5. \u2713\n\n**Vertex in $S_1$, say $v_5 = (1,\\phi,0)$:**\n- $|v_5 - v_1|^2 = 4$. \u2713 ($a$)\n- $|v_5 - v_2|^2 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_2 \\in S_2$)\n- $|v_5 - v_3|^2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_5 - v_4|^2 = 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_5 - v_6|^2 = 0 + (2\\phi)^2 + 0 = 4\\phi^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $|v_5 - v_7|^2 = 4 + 0 + 0 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_7 \\in S_1$)\n- $|v_5 - v_8|^2 = 4 + (2\\phi)^2 + 0 = 4+4\\phi^2 = 8+4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_5 - v_9|^2 = (\\phi-1)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = \\phi^2-2\\phi+1+\\phi^2+1 = 2\\phi^2-2\\phi+2 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_9 \\in S_1$)\n- $|v_5 - v_{10}|^2 = (\\phi-1)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_{10} \\in S_2$)\n- $|v_5 - v_{11}|^2 = (\\phi+1)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $|v_5 - v_{12}|^2 = (\\phi+1)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n\nSo $v_5$ is adjacent to: $v_1 (=a)$, $v_7, v_9$ (in $S_1$), $v_2, v_{10}$ (in $S_2$).\nThat's 1 in $\\{a\\}$, 2 in $S_1$, 2 in $S_2$, 0 in $\\{a'\\}$. Total: 5. \u2713\n\nGreat, so for any vertex in $S_1$: 1 neighbor in $\\{a\\}$, 2 in $S_1$, 2 in $S_2$, 0 in $\\{a'\\}$.\n\n**Vertex in $S_2$, say $v_2 = (0,1,-\\phi)$:**\n- $|v_2 - v_1|^2 = 4\\phi^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $|v_2 - v_3|^2 = 4+4\\phi^2 = 8+4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_2 - v_4|^2 = 4$. \u2713 ($a'$)\n- $|v_2 - v_5|^2 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_5 \\in S_1$)\n- $|v_2 - v_6|^2 = 1 + (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_2 - v_7|^2 = 1 + (1-\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_7 \\in S_1$)\n- $|v_2 - v_8|^2 = 1 + (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_2 - v_9|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $|v_2 - v_{10}|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_{10} \\in S_2$)\n- $|v_2 - v_{11}|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $|v_2 - v_{12}|^2 = \\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_{12} \\in S_2$)\n\nSo $v_2$ is adjacent to: $v_4 (=a')$, $v_5, v_7$ (in $S_1$), $v_{10}, v_{12}$ (in $S_2$).\nThat's 0 in $\\{a\\}$, 2 in $S_1$, 2 in $S_2$, 1 in $\\{a'\\}$. Total: 5. \u2713\n\n**Vertex in $S_2$, say $v_6 = (1,-\\phi,0)$:**\n- $|v_6 - v_1|^2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $|v_6 - v_3|^2 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_3 \\in S_1$)\n- $|v_6 - v_4|^2 = 1 + (1-\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4$. \u2713 ($a'$)\n\nWait, let me recompute. $v_6 = (1,-\\phi,0)$, $v_4 = (0,-1,-\\phi)$.\n$|v_6 - v_4|^2 = 1 + (-\\phi+1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + \\phi^2 - 2\\phi + 1 + \\phi^2 = 2\\phi^2 - 2\\phi + 2 = 4$. \u2713\n\n- $|v_6 - v_5|^2 = 4\\phi^2$. No.\n- $|v_6 - v_7|^2 = 4 + 0 + 0 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_7 \\in S_1$)\n- $|v_6 - v_8|^2 = 0 + (2\\phi)^2 + 0 = 4\\phi^2$. No.\n- $|v_6 - v_9|^2 = (\\phi-1)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_9 \\in S_1$)\n\nWait, that gives 3 in $S_1$. Let me recheck.\n\n$v_6 = (1,-\\phi,0)$, $v_9 = (\\phi,0,1)$.\n$|v_6 - v_9|^2 = (1-\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = (1-2\\phi+\\phi^2) + \\phi^2 + 1 = 2 - 2\\phi + 2\\phi^2 = 2 - 2\\phi + 2\\phi + 2 = 4$. \u2713\n\n- $|v_6 - v_{10}|^2 = (1-\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = 4$. \u2713 ($v_{10} \\in S_2$)\n- $|v_6 - v_{11}|^2 = (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = 4\\phi + 4$. No.\n- $|v_6 - v_{12}|^2 = (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = 4\\phi + 4$. No.\n\nSo $v_6$ is adjacent to: $v_3, v_7, v_9$ (in $S_1$), $v_{10}$ (in $S_2$), $v_4 (=a')$.\nThat's 0 in $\\{a\\}$, 3 in $S_1$, 1 in $S_2$, 1 in $\\{a'\\}$. Total: 5.\n\nHmm, this is different from $v_2$! $v_2$ had 2 in $S_1$, 2 in $S_2$, 1 in $\\{a'\\}$, but $v_6$ has 3 in $S_1$, 1 in $S_2$, 1 in $\\{a'\\}$.\n\nThis means the lumping by distance from $a$ is NOT valid! The vertices in $S_2$ are not all equivalent with respect to the transition probabilities to the distance classes.\n\nThis is a problem. I need a finer partition.\n\nLet me reconsider. The stabilizer of $a$ in the icosahedral group is $D_5$ (dihedral group of order 10). The orbits of $D_5$ on the other 11 vertices are the distance classes: $S_1$ (5 vertices), $S_2$ (5 vertices), $\\{a'\\}$ (1 vertex). But the lumping is valid only if vertices in the same orbit have the same number of neighbors in each orbit.\n\nSince $v_2$ and $v_6$ (both in $S_2$) have different numbers of neighbors in each class, the lumping is not valid. This means I need to use a finer partition.\n\nActually wait, I think I made an error. Let me recheck $v_6$'s neighbors.\n\n$v_6 = (1, -\\phi, 0)$.\n\nLet me be more careful:\n- $v_1 = (0, 1, \\phi)$: $|v_6 - v_1|^2 = 1 + (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + 1+2\\phi+\\phi^2+\\phi^2 = 2+2\\phi+2(\\phi+1) = 4+4\\phi$. Not adjacent.\n- $v_2 = (0, 1, -\\phi)$: $|v_6 - v_2|^2 = 1 + (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4+4\\phi$. Not adjacent.\n- $v_3 = (0, -1, \\phi)$: $|v_6 - v_3|^2 = 1 + (1-\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + (1-2\\phi+\\phi^2) + \\phi^2 = 2-2\\phi+2\\phi^2 = 2-2\\phi+2\\phi+2 = 4$. Adjacent! \u2713\n- $v_4 = (0, -1, -\\phi)$: $|v_6 - v_4|^2 = 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + (1-2\\phi+\\phi^2) + \\phi^2 = 4$. Adjacent! \u2713\n- $v_5 = (1, \\phi, 0)$: $|v_6 - v_5|^2 = 0 + (2\\phi)^2 + 0 = 4\\phi^2 = 4\\phi+4$. Not adjacent.\n- $v_7 = (-1, \\phi, 0)$: $|v_6 - v_7|^2 = 4 + (2\\phi)^2 + 0 = 4+4\\phi^2 = 8+4\\phi$. Not adjacent.\n\nWait, I think I made an error. $v_6 = (1,-\\phi,0)$ and $v_7 = (-1,\\phi,0)$.\n$|v_6 - v_7|^2 = (1-(-1))^2 + (-\\phi-\\phi)^2 + 0 = 4 + 4\\phi^2 = 4 + 4\\phi + 4 = 8+4\\phi$. Not adjacent.\n\nLet me redo:\n- $v_8 = (-1, -\\phi, 0)$: $|v_6 - v_8|^2 = 4 + 0 + 0 = 4$. Adjacent! \u2713\n- $v_9 = (\\phi, 0, 1)$: $|v_6 - v_9|^2 = (1-\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = (1-2\\phi+\\phi^2) + \\phi^2 + 1 = 2-2\\phi+2\\phi^2 = 4$. Adjacent! \u2713\n- $v_{10} = (\\phi, 0, -1)$: $|v_6 - v_{10}|^2 = (1-\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = 4$. Adjacent! \u2713\n- $v_{11} = (-\\phi, 0, 1)$: $|v_6 - v_{11}|^2 = (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = (1+2\\phi+\\phi^2)+\\phi^2+1 = 2+2\\phi+2\\phi^2 = 2+2\\phi+2\\phi+2 = 4+4\\phi$. Not adjacent.\n- $v_{12} = (-\\phi, 0, -1)$: $|v_6 - v_{12}|^2 = (1+\\phi)^2 + \\phi^2 + 1 = 4+4\\phi$. Not adjacent.\n\nSo $v_6$ is adjacent to: $v_3, v_8$ (in $S_1$), $v_9$ (in $S_1$)... \n\nWait, $v_8 = (-1,-\\phi,0)$. Is $v_8$ in $S_1$? $S_1 = \\{v_3, v_5, v_7, v_9, v_{11}\\}$. No, $v_8$ is in $S_2 = \\{v_2, v_6, v_8, v_{10}, v_{12}\\}$.\n\nSo $v_6$ is adjacent to:\n- $v_3 \\in S_1$\n- $v_4 = a'$\n- $v_8 \\in S_2$\n- $v_9 \\in S_1$\n- $v_{10} \\in S_2$\n\nThat's 2 in $S_1$, 2 in $S_2$, 1 in $\\{a'\\}$. Total: 5. \u2713\n\nI made an error before when I said $v_7$ was a neighbor. Let me recheck: $v_6 = (1,-\\phi,0)$, $v_7 = (-1,\\phi,0)$.\n$|v_6-v_7|^2 = 4 + 4\\phi^2 + 0 = 4+4\\phi+4 = 8+4\\phi$. Definitely not adjacent.\n\nSo I made an arithmetic error earlier. Let me redo $v_2$ as well to be sure.\n\n$v_2 = (0,1,-\\phi)$:\n- $v_1 = (0,1,\\phi)$: $|v_2-v_1|^2 = 0+0+4\\phi^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $v_3 = (0,-1,\\phi)$: $|v_2-v_3|^2 = 0+4+4\\phi^2 = 4+4\\phi+4 = 8+4\\phi$. No.\n- $v_4 = (0,-1,-\\phi)$: $|v_2-v_4|^2 = 0+4+0 = 4$. Adjacent! ($a'$)\n- $v_5 = (1,\\phi,0)$: $|v_2-v_5|^2 = 1+(1-\\phi)^2+\\phi^2 = 1+1-2\\phi+\\phi^2+\\phi^2 = 2-2\\phi+2\\phi+2 = 4$. Adjacent! ($S_1$)\n- $v_6 = (1,-\\phi,0)$: $|v_2-v_6|^2 = 1+(1+\\phi)^2+\\phi^2 = 1+1+2\\phi+\\phi^2+\\phi^2 = 2+2\\phi+2\\phi+2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $v_7 = (-1,\\phi,0)$: $|v_2-v_7|^2 = 1+(1-\\phi)^2+\\phi^2 = 4$. Adjacent! ($S_1$)\n- $v_8 = (-1,-\\phi,0)$: $|v_2-v_8|^2 = 1+(1+\\phi)^2+\\phi^2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $v_9 = (\\phi,0,1)$: $|v_2-v_9|^2 = \\phi^2+1+(\\phi+1)^2 = \\phi^2+1+\\phi^2+2\\phi+1 = 2\\phi^2+2\\phi+2 = 2\\phi+2+2\\phi+2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $v_{10} = (\\phi,0,-1)$: $|v_2-v_{10}|^2 = \\phi^2+1+(\\phi-1)^2 = \\phi^2+1+\\phi^2-2\\phi+1 = 2\\phi^2-2\\phi+2 = 2\\phi+2-2\\phi+2 = 4$. Adjacent! ($S_2$)\n- $v_{11} = (-\\phi,0,1)$: $|v_2-v_{11}|^2 = \\phi^2+1+(\\phi+1)^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $v_{12} = (-\\phi,0,-1)$: $|v_2-v_{12}|^2 = \\phi^2+1+(\\phi-1)^2 = 4$. Adjacent! ($S_2$)\n\nSo $v_2$ is adjacent to: $v_4 (=a')$, $v_5, v_7$ (in $S_1$), $v_{10}, v_{12}$ (in $S_2$).\nThat's 0 in $\\{a\\}$, 2 in $S_1$, 2 in $S_2$, 1 in $\\{a'\\}$. Total: 5. \u2713\n\nAnd $v_6$ is adjacent to: $v_3, v_9$ (in $S_1$), $v_8, v_{10}$ (in $S_2$), $v_4 (=a')$.\nThat's 0 in $\\{a\\}$, 2 in $S_1$, 2 in $S_2$, 1 in $\\{a'\\}$. Total: 5. \u2713\n\nSo actually both $v_2$ and $v_6$ have the same transition structure: 2 in $S_1$, 2 in $S_2$, 1 in $\\{a'\\}$. I must have made an arithmetic error earlier when I computed $v_6$'s neighbors.\n\nLet me now summarize the transition structure:\n\n| Current class | \u2192 $\\{a\\}$ | \u2192 $S_1$ | \u2192 $S_2$ | \u2192 $\\{a'\\}$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| $\\{a\\}$ | 0 | 5 | 0 | 0 |\n| $S_1$ | 1 | 2 | 2 | 0 |\n| $S_2$ | 0 | 2 | 2 | 1 |\n| $\\{a'\\}$ | 0 | 0 | 5 | 0 |\n\nLet me verify $a'$: $a' = v_4 = (0,-1,-\\phi)$.\n- $v_1$: $|v_4-v_1|^2 = 0+4+4\\phi^2 = 8+4\\phi$. No.\n- $v_2$: $|v_4-v_2|^2 = 0+4+0 = 4$. Adjacent! ($S_2$)\n- $v_3$: $|v_4-v_3|^2 = 0+0+4\\phi^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $v_5$: $|v_4-v_5|^2 = 1+(1+\\phi)^2+\\phi^2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $v_6$: $|v_4-v_6|^2 = 1+(\\phi-1)^2+\\phi^2 = 4$. Adjacent! ($S_2$)\n- $v_7$: $|v_4-v_7|^2 = 1+(1-\\phi)^2+\\phi^2 = 4$. Adjacent! ($S_1$)\n\nWait, $v_7 = (-1,\\phi,0)$ is in $S_1$. Let me recheck.\n$v_4 = (0,-1,-\\phi)$, $v_7 = (-1,\\phi,0)$.\n$|v_4-v_7|^2 = 1+(-1-\\phi)^2+\\phi^2 = 1+(1+\\phi)^2+\\phi^2 = 1+1+2\\phi+\\phi^2+\\phi^2 = 2+2\\phi+2\\phi^2 = 2+2\\phi+2\\phi+2 = 4+4\\phi$. Not adjacent!\n\nI keep making errors. Let me be very careful.\n\n$(1+\\phi)^2 = 1 + 2\\phi + \\phi^2 = 1 + 2\\phi + \\phi + 1 = 2 + 3\\phi$.\n\nSo $|v_4 - v_7|^2 = 1 + (2+3\\phi) + \\phi^2 = 1 + 2 + 3\\phi + \\phi + 1 = 4 + 4\\phi$. Not adjacent.\n\nLet me redo all neighbors of $v_4 = (0,-1,-\\phi)$:\n- $v_1 = (0,1,\\phi)$: $0 + 4 + 4\\phi^2 = 4 + 4(\\phi+1) = 8+4\\phi$. No.\n- $v_2 = (0,1,-\\phi)$: $0 + 4 + 0 = 4$. YES.\n- $v_3 = (0,-1,\\phi)$: $0 + 0 + 4\\phi^2 = 4\\phi+4$. No.\n- $v_5 = (1,\\phi,0)$: $1 + (\\phi+1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + (2+3\\phi) + (\\phi+1) = 1+2+3\\phi+\\phi+1 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $v_6 = (1,-\\phi,0)$: $1 + (\\phi-1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 1 + (1-2\\phi+\\phi^2) + \\phi^2 = 2-2\\phi+2\\phi^2 = 2-2\\phi+2\\phi+2 = 4$. YES.\n- $v_7 = (-1,\\phi,0)$: $1 + (\\phi+1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $v_8 = (-1,-\\phi,0)$: $1 + (\\phi-1)^2 + \\phi^2 = 4$. YES.\n- $v_9 = (\\phi,0,1)$: $\\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 = (\\phi+1) + 1 + (2+3\\phi) = \\phi+1+1+2+3\\phi = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $v_{10} = (\\phi,0,-1)$: $\\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = (\\phi+1) + 1 + (2-2\\phi+\\phi+\\phi) ... \n\nWait, $(\\phi-1)^2 = \\phi^2 - 2\\phi + 1 = (\\phi+1) - 2\\phi + 1 = 2 - \\phi$.\n\nSo $\\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = (\\phi+1) + 1 + (2-\\phi) = \\phi + 1 + 1 + 2 - \\phi = 4$. YES.\n\n- $v_{11} = (-\\phi,0,1)$: $\\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi+1)^2 = (\\phi+1) + 1 + (2+3\\phi) = 4+4\\phi$. No.\n- $v_{12} = (-\\phi,0,-1)$: $\\phi^2 + 1 + (\\phi-1)^2 = 4$. YES.\n\nSo $v_4$ is adjacent to: $v_2, v_6, v_8, v_{10}, v_{12}$.\n\nNow, $v_2, v_6, v_8, v_{10}, v_{12}$ are all in $S_2$!\n\nSo $a'$ is adjacent to all 5 vertices in $S_2$. That's consistent with what I expected.\n\nSo the transition table is:\n\n| Current class | \u2192 $\\{a\\}$ | \u2192 $S_1$ | \u2192 $S_2$ | \u2192 $\\{a'\\}$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| $\\{a\\}$ | 0 | 5 | 0 | 0 |\n| $S_1$ | 1 | 2 | 2 | 0 |\n| $S_2$ | 0 | 2 | 2 | 1 |\n| $\\{a'\\}$ | 0 | 0 | 5 | 0 |\n\nNow I can set up the lumped Markov chain. Let:\n- $x_t$ = probability at $a$ at time $t$\n- $y_t$ = probability at a specific vertex in $S_1$ at time $t$ (all 5 are symmetric)\n- $z_t$ = probability at a specific vertex in $S_2$ at time $t$ (all 5 are symmetric)\n- $w_t$ = probability at $a'$ at time $t$\n\nThen $x_t + 5y_t + 5z_t + w_t = 1$.\n\nTransitions (dividing by 5 since each vertex in $S_1$ or $S_2$ has 5 neighbors):\n\n$x_{t+1} = \\frac{1}{5}(5y_t) = y_t$\n\nWait, let me be more careful. The probability of being at $a$ at time $t+1$ is the sum over all vertices $v$ of $P(\\text{at } v \\text{ at time } t) \\cdot P(v \\to a)$.\n\n$P(v \\to a) = \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot (\\text{number of edges from } v \\text{ to } a)$.\n\n- From $a$: 0 edges to $a$.\n- From a vertex in $S_1$: 1 edge to $a$. So $P(v \\to a) = 1/5$.\n- From a vertex in $S_2$: 0 edges to $a$.\n- From $a'$: 0 edges to $a$.\n\nSo $x_{t+1} = \\sum_{v \\in S_1} P_t(v) \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} = 5y_t \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} = y_t$.\n\nSimilarly:\n$w_{t+1} = \\sum_{v \\in S_2} P_t(v) \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} = 5z_t \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} = z_t$.\n\nFor $y_{t+1}$ (probability at a specific vertex, say $v_3$, in $S_1$ at time $t+1$):\n$y_{t+1} = \\sum_{u} P_t(u) \\cdot P(u \\to v_3)$.\n\n$P(u \\to v_3) = \\frac{1}{5}$ if $u$ is adjacent to $v_3$, else 0.\n\nNeighbors of $v_3$: $v_1 (=a)$, $v_6, v_8$ (in $S_2$), $v_9, v_{11}$ (in $S_1$).\n\nSo $y_{t+1} = P_t(a) \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} + P_t(v_6) \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} + P_t(v_8) \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} + P_t(v_9) \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} + P_t(v_{11}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}$\n$= \\frac{1}{5}(x_t + z_t + z_t + y_t + y_t) = \\frac{1}{5}(x_t + 2y_t + 2z_t)$.\n\nFor $z_{t+1}$ (probability at a specific vertex, say $v_2$, in $S_2$ at time $t+1$):\nNeighbors of $v_2$: $v_4 (=a')$, $v_5, v_7$ (in $S_1$), $v_{10}, v_{12}$ (in $S_2$).\n\n$z_{t+1} = \\frac{1}{5}(w_t + 2y_t + 2z_t)$.\n\nSo the system is:\n$$x_{t+1} = y_t$$\n$$y_{t+1} = \\frac{x_t + 2y_t + 2z_t}{5}$$\n$$z_{t+1} = \\frac{w_t + 2y_t + 2z_t}{5}$$\n$$w_{t+1} = z_t$$\n\nwith initial conditions $x_0 = 1, y_0 = z_0 = w_0 = 0$.\n\nThis is a 4-dimensional linear system. Let me write the transition matrix:\n\n$$M = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1/5 & 2/5 & 2/5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2/5 & 2/5 & 1/5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nwhere the state vector is $(x_t, y_t, z_t, w_t)^T$.\n\nI need to find $x_t = (M^t \\mathbf{e}_1)_1$ where $\\mathbf{e}_1 = (1,0,0,0)^T$.\n\nLet me find the eigenvalues of $M$. The characteristic polynomial is $\\det(M - \\lambda I)$.\n\n$$\\det\\begin{pmatrix} -\\lambda & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1/5 & 2/5-\\lambda & 2/5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2/5 & 2/5-\\lambda & 1/5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nLet me expand along the first column:\n$= -\\lambda \\det\\begin{pmatrix} 2/5-\\lambda & 2/5 & 0 \\\\ 2/5 & 2/5-\\lambda & 1/5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{pmatrix} + \\frac{1}{5} \\det\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2/5 & 2/5-\\lambda & 1/5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{pmatrix}$\n\nFirst determinant:\n$\\det\\begin{pmatrix} 2/5-\\lambda & 2/5 & 0 \\\\ 2/5 & 2/5-\\lambda & 1/5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{pmatrix}$\n\n$= (2/5-\\lambda)[(2/5-\\lambda)(-\\lambda) - 1/5] - (2/5)[2/5 \\cdot (-\\lambda) - 0] + 0$\n$= (2/5-\\lambda)[-\\lambda(2/5-\\lambda) - 1/5] - (2/5)(-2\\lambda/5)$\n$= (2/5-\\lambda)[-2\\lambda/5 + \\lambda^2 - 1/5] + 4\\lambda/25$\n$= (2/5-\\lambda)[\\lambda^2 - 2\\lambda/5 - 1/5] + 4\\lambda/25$\n\nLet me compute this: Let $\\mu = 2/5 - \\lambda$. Then $\\lambda = 2/5 - \\mu$.\n$\\lambda^2 - 2\\lambda/5 - 1/5 = (2/5-\\mu)^2 - 2(2/5-\\mu)/5 - 1/5 = 4/25 - 4\\mu/5 + \\mu^2 - 4/25 + 2\\mu/5 - 1/5 = \\mu^2 - 2\\mu/5 - 1/5$.\n\nSo the first determinant $= \\mu(\\mu^2 - 2\\mu/5 - 1/5) + 4(2/5-\\mu)/25 = \\mu^3 - 2\\mu^2/5 - \\mu/5 + 8/25 - 4\\mu/25 = \\mu^3 - 2\\mu^2/5 - \\mu/5 - 4\\mu/25 + 8/25 = \\mu^3 - 2\\mu^2/5 - 9\\mu/25 + 8/25$.\n\nSubstituting back $\\mu = 2/5 - \\lambda$:\nThis is getting messy. Let me try a different approach.\n\nSecond determinant:\n$\\det\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2/5 & 2/5-\\lambda & 1/5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{pmatrix} = 1 \\cdot [(2/5-\\lambda)(-\\lambda) - 1/5] = -\\lambda(2/5-\\lambda) - 1/5 = \\lambda^2 - 2\\lambda/5 - 1/5$.\n\nSo the characteristic polynomial is:\n$-\\lambda \\cdot [\\text{first det}] + \\frac{1}{5}[\\lambda^2 - 2\\lambda/5 - 1/5] = 0$\n\nLet me try a slightly different approach. Instead of computing the full characteristic polynomial, let me use the symmetry to reduce the problem.\n\nActually, let me try to find the eigenvalues more cleverly. The icosahedron is a distance-regular graph with intersection array $\\{5, 2, 1, 1; 1, 1, 2, 5\\}$. Wait, let me recall.\n\nThe icosahedron has 12 vertices, degree 5, diameter 3. The intersection array is $\\{b_0, b_1, b_2, b_3; c_1, c_2, c_3, c_4\\} = \\{5, 2, 1, 1; 1, 1, 2, 5\\}$.\n\nWait, for a distance-regular graph with diameter $D=3$:\n- $b_0 = k = 5$ (degree)\n- $c_1 = 1$\n- $b_1 + c_1 + a_1 = k$, so $b_1 + a_1 = 4$. For the icosahedron, $a_1 = 2$ (each neighbor of $a$ is connected to 2 other neighbors of $a$), so $b_1 = 2$.\n- $b_2 + c_2 + a_2 = k$, so $b_2 + c_2 + a_2 = 5$.\n- $b_3 + c_3 + a_3 = k$, so $b_3 + c_3 + a_3 = 5$.\n\nFor the icosahedron, the distance-2 vertices from $a$ form a pentagon (5-cycle), so $a_2 = 2$, and $b_2 = 1$ (each distance-2 vertex is connected to exactly 1 distance-3 vertex, which is $a'$), $c_2 = 2$ (each distance-2 vertex is connected to 2 distance-1 vertices).\n\nCheck: $b_2 + c_2 + a_2 = 1 + 2 + 2 = 5$. \u2713\n\nFor distance 3: $a_3 = 0$ (only one vertex at distance 3), $b_3 = 0$ (no distance-4 vertices), $c_3 = 5$ (the distance-3 vertex is connected to all 5 distance-2 vertices).\n\nCheck: $b_3 + c_3 + a_3 = 0 + 5 + 0 = 5$. \u2713\n\nSo the intersection array is $\\{5, 2, 1, 0; 1, 2, 5, \\text{N/A}\\}$... \n\nActually, I realize the standard notation is: for a vertex at distance $i$ from $a$, it has $c_i$ neighbors at distance $i-1$, $a_i$ neighbors at distance $i$, and $b_i$ neighbors at distance $i+1$.\n\nFor the icosahedron:\n- $i=0$: $c_0 = 0$, $a_0 = 0$, $b_0 = 5$.\n- $i=1$: $c_1 = 1$, $a_1 = 2$, $b_1 = 2$.\n- $i=2$: $c_2 = 2$, $a_2 = 2$, $b_2 = 1$.\n- $i=3$: $c_3 = 5$, $a_3 = 0$, $b_3 = 0$.\n\nThe transition matrix on the distance classes (dividing by $k=5$) is:\n\n$$T = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1/5 & 2/5 & 2/5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2/5 & 2/5 & 1/5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nThis is the same matrix $M$ I had before! Good.\n\nNow, for distance-regular graphs, the eigenvalues can be computed from the intersection array. The eigenvalues $\\theta_i$ (for $i = 0, 1, \\ldots, D$) satisfy:\n\n$b_i + c_i + a_i = k$ (which we already used)\n\nAnd the eigenvalues satisfy the recurrence:\n$\\theta_0 = k = 5$ (for the adjacency matrix)\n\nActually, for the random walk transition matrix $P = A/k$, the eigenvalues are $\\theta_i/k$ where $\\theta_i$ are the eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix.\n\nFor the adjacency matrix of a distance-regular graph, the eigenvalues satisfy:\n$\\theta_0 = k$\n$b_0 \\theta_1 + c_0 \\theta_{-1} + a_0 \\theta_0 = \\theta_1 \\cdot k$\n\nActually, the standard formula is: the eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of the adjacency matrix satisfy the equation obtained from the \"recursion\" of the distance-regular graph:\n\nFor $i = 0$: $b_0 = \\lambda_0 = k$ (trivially, since the all-ones vector is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $k$).\n\nFor the other eigenvalues, they satisfy the three-term recurrence:\n$c_i \\theta_{i-1} + a_i \\theta_i + b_i \\theta_{i+1} = \\theta \\cdot (\\text{something})$\n\nActually, I recall that for a distance-regular graph, if $\\theta$ is an eigenvalue of the adjacency matrix, then the sequence $u_i = (\\text{projection of the eigenvalue onto the } i\\text{-th distance class})$ satisfies:\n\n$b_i u_{i+1} + a_i u_i + c_i u_{i-1} = \\theta u_i$\n\nwith $u_0 = 1$ and $u_{-1} = 0$.\n\nSo:\n- $i=0$: $b_0 u_1 + c_0 u_{-1} + a_0 u_0 = \\theta u_0$, i.e., $5u_1 = \\theta$.\n- $i=1$: $b_1 u_2 + a_1 u_1 + c_1 u_0 = \\theta u_1$, i.e., $2u_2 + 2u_1 + 1 = \\theta u_1 = 5u_1$. So $2u_2 = 3u_1 - 1 = 3\\theta/5 - 1$. Thus $u_2 = \\frac{3\\theta/5 - 1}{2} = \\frac{3\\theta - 5}{10}$.\n- $i=2$: $b_2 u_3 + a_2 u_2 + c_2 u_1 = \\theta u_2$, i.e., $u_3 + 2u_2 + 2u_1 = \\theta u_2$.\n  $u_3 = \\theta u_2 - 2u_2 - 2u_1 = (\\theta - 2)u_2 - 2u_1 = (\\theta-2) \\cdot \\frac{3\\theta-5}{10} - \\frac{2\\theta}{5} = \\frac{(\\theta-2)(3\\theta-5)}{10} - \\frac{4\\theta}{10} = \\frac{3\\theta^2 - 5\\theta - 6\\theta + 10 - 4\\theta}{10} = \\frac{3\\theta^2 - 15\\theta + 10}{10}$.\n- $i=3$: $b_3 u_4 + a_3 u_3 + c_3 u_2 = \\theta u_3$. Since $b_3 = 0$, $a_3 = 0$: $c_3 u_2 = \\theta u_3$, i.e., $5u_2 = \\theta u_3$.\n\nSo the condition is: $5 \\cdot \\frac{3\\theta-5}{10} = \\theta \\cdot \\frac{3\\theta^2 - 15\\theta + 10}{10}$\n\n$\\frac{3\\theta - 5}{2} = \\frac{3\\theta^3 - 15\\theta^2 + 10\\theta}{10}$\n\n$10(3\\theta - 5)/2 = 3\\theta^3 - 15\\theta^2 + 10\\theta$\n\n$5(3\\theta - 5) = 3\\theta^3 - 15\\theta^2 + 10\\theta$\n\n$15\\theta - 25 = 3\\theta^3 - 15\\theta^2 + 10\\theta$\n\n$3\\theta^3 - 15\\theta^2 - 5\\theta + 25 = 0$\n\nLet me factor this. We know $\\theta = 5$ is a root (for the all-ones eigenvector).\n$3(125) - 15(25) - 5(5) + 25 = 375 - 375 - 25 + 25 = 0$. \u2713\n\nDivide by $(\\theta - 5)$:\n$3\\theta^3 - 15\\theta^2 - 5\\theta + 25 = (\\theta-5)(3\\theta^2 + 0\\theta - 5) = (\\theta-5)(3\\theta^2 - 5)$.\n\nCheck: $(\\theta-5)(3\\theta^2 - 5) = 3\\theta^3 - 5\\theta - 15\\theta^2 + 25 = 3\\theta^3 - 15\\theta^2 - 5\\theta + 25$. \u2713\n\nSo the eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix are: $5, \\sqrt{5/3}, -\\sqrt{5/3}$.\n\nWait, but the icosahedron has 12 vertic